30. What are Kafka Share Groups or Kafka Queues, and how are they different from normal consumer groups?
    Kafka 4.2 made Share Groups, also called Kafka Queues, production-ready. They provide queue-like cooperative consumption in which multiple consumers can process records from the same partitions using individual acknowledgements and delivery counting. Apache Kafka
    
    Compare:
    Traditional consumer group
    - One partition is assigned to one consumer at a time.
    - Parallelism is bounded by partition count.
    - Ordering within the partition is easier to preserve.
    - Offsets represent group progress.
    Share Group
    - Multiple consumers can cooperatively process records from the same partitions.
    - Individual records can be acknowledged.
    - Better suited to work-queue-style processing.
    - Delivery attempts and acknowledgement semantics become important.
    - Strict partition-order assumptions may not apply in the same way.
    This is one of the most important modern Kafka questions for interviews involving Kafka 4.x.

